[agent:result] stdout: Databricks provides a hosted version of MLflow Model Registry in Unity
[agent:result]   Catalog. Models in Unity Catalog provide centralized access control, auditing,
[agent:result]   lineage, and discovery of ML models across Databricks workspaces.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   An MLflow registered model resides in the third layer of Unity Catalog’s
[agent:result]   three-level namespace. Registered models contain model versions, which
[agent:result]   correspond to actual ML models (MLflow models). Creating new model versions
[agent:result]   currently requires use of the MLflow Python client. Once model versions are
[agent:result]   created, you can load them for batch inference using MLflow Python client
[agent:result]   APIs, or deploy them for real-time serving using Databricks Model Serving.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   All operations on registered models and model versions require USE_CATALOG
[agent:result]   permissions on the enclosing catalog and USE_SCHEMA permissions on the
[agent:result]   enclosing schema. In addition, the following additional privileges are
[agent:result]   required for various operations:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   * To create a registered model, users must additionally have the CREATE_MODEL
[agent:result]   permission on the target schema. * To view registered model or model version
[agent:result]   metadata, model version data files, or invoke a model version, users must
[agent:result]   additionally have the EXECUTE permission on the registered model * To update
[agent:result]   registered model or model version tags, users must additionally have APPLY TAG
[agent:result]   permissions on the registered model * To update other registered model or
[agent:result]   model version metadata (comments, aliases) create a new model version, or
[agent:result]   update permissions on the registered model, users must be owners of the
[agent:result]   registered model.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   Note: The securable type for models is FUNCTION. When using REST APIs (e.g.
[agent:result]   tagging, grants) that specify a securable type, use FUNCTION as the securable
[agent:result]   type.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Usage:
